WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.156 --> 00:00:03.030
SETH VARGO: Hi there.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.280
And welcome to our second
video in the series

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.230
on SRE and DevOps.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.150
My name is Seth, and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.822
at Google focused on
infrastructure and operations.

00:00:11.822 --> 00:00:12.780
LIZ FONG-JONES: And hi.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.320
I'm Liz, a site reliability
engineer, or SRE at Google,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.000
where I teach Google
Cloud customers

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.580
how to build and operate
reliable services.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.380
So Seth, have you ever
run into the problem

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.254
where you're trying to
talk about reliability

00:00:24.254 --> 00:00:27.360
and availability, and you get
so many different definitions it

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.710
makes your head want to spin?

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.210
SETH VARGO: It
happens all the time.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.050
LIZ FONG-JONES: And
even worse, have

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.780
you ever run into the situation
where the developers are trying

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.100
to push out new
features and they're

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.360
breaking things more and more,
and they just won't listen?

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.600
SETH VARGO: It's like
you're in my head.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.200
Have we worked together before?

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.380
My team is constantly putting
out fires, many of which

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.960
end up being bugs in
the developers' code.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.210
But when we try to push
back on the product teams

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.730
to focus on reliability,
they don't agree

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.980
that reliability is an issue.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.930
It's the classic DevOps problem.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.335
Do you have any recommendations?

00:00:56.335 --> 00:00:57.210
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yeah.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.700
This is a really common
problem in the relationship

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.278
between product
developers and operators.

00:01:02.278 --> 00:01:04.319
And it's a problem that
Google was really worried

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:06.480
about in the early
2000s when we were first

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.460
building out Google web search.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.460
And this is when we started
defining the SRE discipline.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.200
And it's really been
a work in progress

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.114
that we're improving on since.

00:01:15.114 --> 00:01:15.780
SETH VARGO: Wow.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.780
Google had these same
problems in the early 2000s?

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.310
I had no idea.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.780
But I still don't understand.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.360
How can SRE help solve
this, apparently,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.100
very common problem?

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.800
LIZ FONG-JONES: So SRE tries
to solve this problem in three

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.860
different ways.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.570
First of all, we try to
define what availability is.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.320
Secondly, we try to define
what an appropriate level

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.680
of availability is.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.660
And third, we get
everyone on the same page

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.520
about what we are
going to do if we fail

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.050
to live up to those standards.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.950
And we try to communicate this
across all the organization,

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.055
from product developers
to SREs, and all

00:01:49.055 --> 00:01:51.180
the way from individual
contributors all the way up

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.470
to vice presidents.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.690
That way, we have a shared
sense of responsibility

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.310
for the service and
what we're going

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.980
to do if we need to slow down.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.710
And we do that by defining
service level objectives

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.380
in collaboration with
the product owners.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.810
And by agreeing on these
metrics in advance,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.210
we make sure that there's
less of a chance of confusion

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:10.919
and conflict in the future.

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:13.260
SETH VARGO: OK, so an
SLO is just an agreement

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.410
among stakeholders about how
reliable a service should be.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.595
But shouldn't services just
always be 100% reliable?

00:02:19.595 --> 00:02:20.970
LIZ FONG-JONES:
So the problem is

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.600
that the cost and the technical
complexity of making services

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.820
more reliable gets higher
and higher the closer to 100%

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:28.830
you try to get.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.050
It winds up being the case
that every application

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.860
has a unique set of requirements
that dictate how reliable does

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.870
it have to be before
customers no longer notice

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:37.680
the difference?

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.180
And that means that
we can make sure

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.520
that we have enough room
for error and enough room

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.470
to roll out features reliably.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:44.220
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.678
We should probably
do another video

00:02:45.678 --> 00:02:48.120
where we talk about why
100% availability isn't

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.140
a real target.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.560
OK Liz, I'm ready.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.030
I've decided that I want my
service to be 99.9% reliable.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.260
So where do I get started?

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.480
Do I use Vim, Emacs, Nano?

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:58.500
What do I do?

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.000
LIZ FONG-JONES: So
I'm a Nano user.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.270
But first, you
really have to define

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.910
what availability is
in addition to defining

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.800
how available you want to be.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.580
We need to make sure that we
understand what availability

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.290
is in the [INAUDIBLE]
server service,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.260
and that we have clear numerical
indicators for defining

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.486
that availability.

00:03:17.486 --> 00:03:19.110
And the way that we
go about doing that

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.050
is by defining not just service
level objectives, but service

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.690
level indicators, or SLIs.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.570
So SLIs are most often
metrics over time,

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:30.840
such as request
latency, the throughput

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.570
of requests per second in
a case of a batch system,

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.150
or failures per total
number of requests.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.710
They're usually
aggregated over time,

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.290
and we typically apply a
function like a percentile,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.540
like a 99th percentile
or a median.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.840
And that way, we can get to
a concrete threshold which

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.290
we can define to say, is this
single number good or bad?

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.690
So for instance, a good
service level indicator

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:55.680
might be saying, is the 99th
percentile latency of requests

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.480
received in the
past five minutes

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.970
less than 300 milliseconds?

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.370
Or alternatively, another
service level indicator

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.190
might be, is the ratio of
errors to total requests

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:08.135
received in the past five
minutes less than 1%?

00:04:08.135 --> 00:04:08.760
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:09.780
Thank you for explaining that.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:10.800
It's much clearer now.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.999
But how does that
SLI become an SLO?

00:04:12.999 --> 00:04:15.540
LIZ FONG-JONES: So if you think
back to your calculus lesson,

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.039
Seth--

00:04:16.039 --> 00:04:17.850
I know this may have
been a while ago.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.709
When you have a service
level indicator,

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.380
it says at any moment in
time whether the service

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.510
was available or
whether it was down.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.820
So what we need to do is
we need to add all that up

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.320
or integrate it over a
longer period of time--

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.210
like a year, in your example
of 99.9% over a year--

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.550
to see, is the total
amount of downtime

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.100
that we've had more or
less than the nine hours

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.870
that you were worried about?

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:43.939
SETH VARGO: But you should
always beat your SLO, right?

00:04:43.939 --> 00:04:44.730
LIZ FONG-JONES: No.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:49.540
So the thing is that SLOs are
both upper and lower bounds.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.490
So this is for two reasons.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.550
First of all, the
fact is that if you

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.380
try to run your service
much more reliable

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.047
than it needs to be,
you're slowing down

00:04:57.047 --> 00:04:59.296
the release of features that
you might want to get out

00:04:59.296 --> 00:05:00.960
that would make your
customers happier

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.700
than having an extra
femtosecond of up time.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.250
And then secondly,
it's an expectation

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.140
that you're sending
for your users--

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:09.849
that if you suddenly
start breaking a lot

00:05:09.849 --> 00:05:11.889
more often than they're
used to because you start

00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:13.810
running exactly at your
SLO rather than doing

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.060
much better than your
SLO, then your users

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.890
will be unhappily
surprised if they're

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.055
trying to build other
services on top of yours.

00:05:20.055 --> 00:05:20.680
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.410
So this is all starting to
make a little bit of sense now.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.340
But what is an SLA then?

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.880
There are so many
SL letter things.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.620
And I remember at a previous
job, I signed an SLA something.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:33.880
What did I do?

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.710
LIZ FONG-JONES: So to
spell it out first,

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:38.560
a SLA is a service
level agreement.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.780
And what it does
is it says, here's

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.530
what I am going to do if I don't
meet the level of reliability

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:45.730
that is expected.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:47.950
It's more of a
commercial agreement that

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:49.810
describes what
remediation you're

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:52.120
going to take if your
service is out of spec

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.320
according to the contract.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:54.070
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:56.590
So the SLA is like
a business agreement

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:57.970
associated with an SLO.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:59.920
So they're exactly
the same, right?

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:01.960
LIZ FONG-JONES: Not
quite, because you really

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.380
want to make your SLA more
lenient than your SLO.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.440
So you get early
warning before you

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.720
have to do things like
field angry phone calls

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:12.820
from customers or
have to pay them

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:16.030
lots of money for failing to
deliver the services promised.

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:18.520
We rarely work with SREs.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:22.330
And instead, we focus on meeting
our SLOs with the understanding

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:24.640
that sales teams and business
teams will think more

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.980
about the SLAs they
build on top of our SLOs.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:27.730
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:30.970
So SLAs describe the set of
services and availability

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:34.270
promises that a provider is
willing to make to a customer,

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:36.220
and then the
ramifications associated

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.500
with failing to deliver
on those promises.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.900
Those ramifications might
be things like money back

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.660
or free credits for failing
to deliver the service

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:45.070
availability.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.230
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yes,
that's exactly correct.

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.630
SETH VARGO: So to
summarize, SLIs

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.580
are service level
indicators or metrics

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:55.170
over time, which inform about
the health of a service.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.910
SLOs are service
level objectives,

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:01.390
which are agreed upon bounds
for how often those SLIs must

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:03.070
be met.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:06.880
And finally, SLAs are
business level agreements,

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:08.740
which define the
service availability

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.530
for a customer and the
penalties for failing

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.170
to deliver that availability.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:14.170
LIZ FONG-JONES: Exactly.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:17.800
So SLIs, SLOs, and
SLAs hearken very much

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:21.580
to the DevOps principle that
measurement is critical,

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:23.370
and that the easiest
way to break down

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.000
the organizational
barriers is to have

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.510
common language about what
it means to be available.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:31.690
And we give, with SLIs, a
very well-defined numerical

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:33.850
measurement for what that is.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:36.820
And with the SLOs, we
collaborate between the product

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:40.060
owners and the SREs
in order to make sure

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:42.460
that the service is running
at an appropriate level

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.440
of reliability for customers.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:46.690
It's a lot clearer
to me now why we say

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:49.157
class SRE implements DevOps.

00:07:49.157 --> 00:07:49.990
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:50.740
Thanks, Liz.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.080
And thank you for watching.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.830
Be sure to check out
the description below

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:54.970
for more information.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:57.040
And don't forget to
subscribe to the channel

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:58.960
and stay tuned for our
next video, where we

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.950
discuss risk and error budgets.

